\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Notation}
\title{Notation for lattice fermions}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{The Berezin integral is #{\int\extd\bar\psi\,\extd\psi}
([ordered Berezin coefficients](fdip-008F), \ref{fdip-00RN}).}

\p{The paired generator is #{\bar\psi}
(\ref{fdip-008H}, \ref{fdip-00RO}).}

\p{The spinor factor carries #{\varepsilon_j^{S}}, with
#{\bbI_F} on the internal factor
([covariant centered differences](fdip-009O), \ref{fdip-00RP}).}

\p{Chirality is #{C=\ui^m\psI} when the ordered product is the
volume element #{\psI}, and #{(-1)^{\deg}} when the grading is
not that volume element
(\ref{fdip-009X}, \ref{fdip-00RQ}).}

\p{Grassmann integration is written like ordinary integration,
#{\int\extd\bar\psi\,\extd\psi}, sharing the measure #{\extd} of
[Gaussian laws](fdip-002B) to mean top-degree extraction. The paired generator
#{\bar\psi} keeps the bar because it is the image of #{\psi}
under the declared antilinear reflection. The image is an independent
generator, not the complex conjugate of a coefficient
([ordered Berezin coefficients](fdip-008F)). The reflection reuses
the involution of \ref{fdip-00QP}, with no matrix entering. Gamma
matrices do not appear: the spinor factor carries
#{\varepsilon_j^{S}=\rho(\iota(\varepsilon_j))}, the represented
generator of the [centered Dirac operator](fdip-000R). The grid step
keeps #{e_j}, and chirality is #{C=\ui^m\psI} when the ordered
product is the volume element #{\psI}, or the degree involution
#{(-1)^{\deg}}.}
